"""Dataset v2 hand-written topics: GENERAL FITNESS (the weakest area in the v1 test suites).

Each item: (category, intent, [question paraphrases], answer). Answers are general guidance consistent with
data/knowledge/knowledge_base.json (WHO / ACSM-style recommendations); no exact food numbers here.
build_dataset_v2.py adds casual / typo variants of the questions automatically.
"""

G = "healthy_lifestyle"
T = [
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it okay to work out every day?", "Can I exercise every single day?", "Is daily training bad for me?"],
     "Light activity like walking or easy cycling is fine every day. For hard strength or high-intensity sessions, give each muscle group about 48 hours before training it hard again, so alternate hard and easy days or split muscle groups across the week. Plan at least one rest or very light day per week, and back off if you feel run down, sore for days, or your performance drops."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is progressive overload?", "Can you explain progressive overload?", "How does progressive overload work?"],
     "Progressive overload means gradually asking your body to do a bit more over time so it keeps adapting. You can add a little weight, do one or two more reps, add a set, slow down the lowering phase, or shorten rest. Change one thing at a time, keep good form, and aim for small steady progress - for example adding reps until you hit the top of your range, then adding weight."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How much exercise do adults need per week?", "How many minutes of exercise should I get a week?", "What are the exercise guidelines for adults?"],
     "General guidelines for adults are 150-300 minutes of moderate activity (like brisk walking) or 75-150 minutes of vigorous activity (like running) per week, plus muscle-strengthening exercises for all major muscle groups on at least 2 days. Any amount is better than none, so if you're starting out, build up gradually."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is the best time of day to exercise?", "Is it better to work out in the morning or evening?", "When should I train during the day?"],
     "The best time is the one you can stick to consistently. Some people feel stronger later in the day, while morning workouts are easier to protect from a busy schedule. Avoid very intense sessions right before bed if they disturb your sleep, and give yourself time to warm up properly, especially early in the morning."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it bad to skip leg day?", "Do I really need to train legs?", "What happens if I never train legs?"],
     "Skipping legs means missing some of your biggest muscles - quads, hamstrings, glutes and calves. Training them builds overall strength, supports your knees and back, helps everyday movements like climbing stairs, and burns more energy per session. Include at least one or two leg-focused movements, such as squats, lunges or hip hinges, each week."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What are the benefits of strength training?", "Why should I lift weights?", "Is strength training worth it?"],
     "Strength training builds and maintains muscle, makes everyday tasks easier, strengthens bones and joints, improves posture and balance, and helps with blood sugar control and body composition. It becomes even more important with age, because muscle mass naturally declines. Two to three full-body sessions a week are enough to get most of these benefits."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I get fit fast?", "What's the quickest way to get in shape?", "How can I get fit quickly?"],
     "There's no shortcut, but you can make fast, safe progress by training consistently: 2-3 strength sessions a week covering the whole body, 2-3 cardio sessions mixing easy and harder efforts, daily walking, enough protein, and 7-9 hours of sleep. Start at a level you can recover from - doing too much too soon usually leads to injury or burnout, which slows you down."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is swimming a good workout?", "Does swimming count as exercise?", "Is swimming good for fitness?"],
     "Yes - swimming is an excellent full-body workout. It trains your heart and lungs, works the back, shoulders, arms, core and legs, and is low impact, so it's kind to the joints. It counts toward your weekly cardio. Because the water supports your body weight, add some land-based strength training too for bone health."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Do I need a personal trainer?", "Is a personal trainer worth it?", "Should I hire a coach?"],
     "You don't need one, but a qualified trainer can help a lot if you're new, unsure about technique, returning from injury, or stuck on a plateau. Even a few sessions to learn the main lifts and get a plan can be worth it. If you train alone, follow a simple structured program and film your sets to check your form."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I stay motivated to exercise?", "I keep losing motivation to work out", "How can I stay consistent with training?"],
     "Motivation comes and goes, so build habits instead: schedule workouts like appointments, start with sessions short enough that you can't say no, track your progress, and pick activities you actually enjoy. Training with a friend or class adds accountability. Set small goals, celebrate them, and on low-energy days do a shorter session rather than skipping completely."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How long does it take to see results from working out?", "When will I see results from the gym?", "How soon do workouts show results?"],
     "You'll usually feel better - more energy, better sleep and mood - within 2-4 weeks. Strength often improves noticeably in the first 4-8 weeks as your nervous system learns the movements. Visible changes in muscle or body fat typically take 8-12 weeks of consistent training and good nutrition. Track measurements, photos and strength rather than only the scale."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a good warm-up before a workout?", "How should I warm up?", "How long should a warm-up be?"],
     "Spend 5-10 minutes warming up: a few minutes of light cardio to raise your heart rate, then dynamic movements like leg swings, arm circles, hip openers and bodyweight squats. Finish with one or two lighter sets of your first exercise. Save long static stretches for after the workout."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Should I do cardio or weights first?", "Cardio before or after lifting?", "What order should I do cardio and strength?"],
     "Do whichever matters most for your goal first, while you're fresh. If you mainly want strength or muscle, lift first and do cardio after or on another day. If you're training for endurance, do the cardio first. A short easy cardio warm-up before lifting is fine either way."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How many sets and reps should I do?", "What rep range is best?", "How many reps should I do per set?"],
     "For general fitness and muscle growth, 2-4 sets of 8-12 reps works well. For strength, heavier sets of 3-6 reps; for muscular endurance, 12-20 reps with lighter weights. Choose a weight that leaves about 1-3 reps in reserve at the end of each set, and rest 60-120 seconds between sets (longer for heavy lifts)."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How long should my workout be?", "How long should I spend at the gym?", "Is a 30 minute workout enough?"],
     "Most effective workouts take 30-60 minutes. Even 20-30 focused minutes of full-body strength or interval training is worthwhile, especially if you train regularly. Longer isn't automatically better - quality, consistency and recovery matter more than time spent."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How many times a week should I work out?", "How often should I exercise?", "How many training days per week?"],
     "For most people, 3-5 days a week works well: for example 2-3 strength sessions and 2-3 cardio sessions, with daily walking. Beginners can get great results from 3 full-body sessions a week. Make sure each muscle group gets about 48 hours to recover after a hard session."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a plateau and how do I break it?", "My progress has stopped, what should I do?", "I'm stuck at the same weights"],
     "A plateau is when progress stalls for several weeks. Check the basics first: sleep, protein and total food intake, and stress. Then change one training variable - add a set, switch the rep range, swap an exercise for a similar one, or take a lighter deload week before pushing again. Track your workouts so you can see what's actually changing."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a deload week?", "Do I need a deload?", "How do deload weeks work?"],
     "A deload is a planned easier week - usually every 4-8 weeks of hard training - where you cut volume or weight by roughly 30-50% while keeping the same exercises. It lets accumulated fatigue fade so you come back stronger. Consider one if you feel run down, sleep poorly or your lifts stall for a while."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What's the difference between strength and endurance training?", "Strength vs endurance training?"],
     "Strength training uses heavier loads for fewer reps to build maximal force and muscle, while endurance training uses lighter effort for longer - many reps or long cardio sessions - to improve stamina and the heart and lungs. Most people benefit from a mix of both across the week."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is walking good exercise?", "Does walking count as a workout?", "Can walking keep me fit?"],
     "Yes - brisk walking is one of the easiest and most sustainable forms of moderate exercise. It counts toward the 150-300 minutes of weekly activity, supports heart health, mood and weight management, and is gentle on the joints. Make it harder with hills, a faster pace or longer distances, and add strength training twice a week."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How many steps should I walk a day?", "Is 10,000 steps a day necessary?", "How many steps per day is healthy?"],
     "10,000 steps is a popular target rather than a strict rule. Benefits rise steadily as you move from very few steps toward roughly 7,000-10,000 a day, and older adults benefit at somewhat lower counts. If you're at 3,000 now, aim to add 1,000-2,000 steps and build from there."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it too late to start exercising at 50?", "Am I too old to start working out?", "Can older people start lifting weights?"],
     "It's never too late. Adults of any age gain strength, balance, mobility and heart health from regular exercise. Start gently, focus on good technique, include strength and balance work, and build up gradually. If you have a medical condition or haven't exercised in years, check with your doctor first."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Can I build muscle and lose fat at the same time?", "Is body recomposition possible?", "Can I lose fat and gain muscle together?"],
     "Yes, especially if you're new to training, returning after a break, or have more body fat to lose. Lift weights with progressive overload, eat plenty of protein (around 1.6 g per kg of body weight per day), keep only a small calorie deficit, and sleep well. Progress is slower than focusing on one goal, so judge it by strength and measurements, not just the scale."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is HIIT?", "Is HIIT better than steady cardio?", "What are the benefits of interval training?"],
     "HIIT (high-intensity interval training) alternates short, hard efforts with recovery periods - for example 30 seconds fast, 60-90 seconds easy, repeated 6-10 times. It's time-efficient and improves fitness quickly, but it's demanding, so 1-3 sessions a week is plenty. Steady, moderate cardio is easier to recover from and great for building a base; most people do well with a mix."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What are compound movements?", "Which exercises work the most muscles?", "Why are compound lifts important?"],
     "Compound movements use several joints and muscle groups at once - squats, deadlifts, lunges, push-ups, presses, rows and pull-ups. They build the most overall strength and are time-efficient, so base your workouts on them and add isolation exercises like curls or lateral raises for extra focus."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is functional fitness?", "What does functional training mean?"],
     "Functional fitness trains movements you use in daily life - squatting, hinging, pushing, pulling, carrying, lunging and rotating - often with free weights, bodyweight or kettlebells. It improves strength, balance and coordination for real-world tasks like lifting groceries or climbing stairs."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Should I train to failure?", "Is training to failure necessary?", "How close to failure should I train?"],
     "You don't need to reach failure on every set. Most sets should end with about 1-3 reps left in reserve, which gives nearly the same muscle-building benefit with less fatigue and injury risk. Occasional failure on safer exercises (like machines or curls) is fine, but avoid it on heavy squats, deadlifts or presses without a spotter."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How long should I rest between sets?", "What rest time is best between sets?"],
     "Rest about 60-90 seconds for most muscle-building work, 2-3 minutes (or more) for heavy compound lifts like squats and deadlifts, and 30-60 seconds for light endurance or circuit work. Rest long enough that you can keep good form on the next set."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is muscle soreness a sign of a good workout?", "Do I need to be sore to grow?", "Why am I not sore anymore?"],
     "No - soreness (DOMS) mostly shows that you did something new or more than usual, not that the workout was better. As your body adapts you'll feel less sore even while you keep improving. Judge progress by strength, reps and consistency rather than how sore you are."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What should I do on rest days?", "How do I spend a rest day?", "Is active recovery useful?"],
     "Rest days can be fully restful or include light active recovery like walking, easy cycling, swimming, mobility work or gentle stretching. Keep the intensity low so you feel better afterwards, not more tired. Focus on sleep, hydration and good meals so your muscles can recover."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I track my fitness progress?", "What's the best way to measure progress?", "How can I tell if I'm getting fitter?"],
     "Keep a simple training log of exercises, weights, sets and reps - steadily adding reps or weight is the clearest sign of progress. Add regular measurements (waist, photos, body weight trends), cardio markers like how far you go in 20 minutes or your resting heart rate, and notes on energy and sleep."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Can I work out with sore muscles?", "Should I train if I'm still sore?", "Is it OK to exercise when sore?"],
     "Mild soreness is fine - light movement and training a different muscle group can even help you feel better. If a muscle is very sore, give it another day before training it hard again. Sharp pain, swelling or pain in a joint is different and should be checked rather than trained through."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it bad to exercise when I'm sick?", "Can I work out with a cold?", "Should I train when I have a fever?"],
     "A common guide is the 'neck check': with mild symptoms above the neck (runny nose, mild sore throat) gentle exercise is usually OK if you feel up to it. With a fever, body aches, chest symptoms or stomach illness, rest until you've recovered, then ease back in. If symptoms are severe or don't improve, see a doctor."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is mobility training?", "Mobility vs flexibility - what's the difference?", "Do I need mobility work?"],
     "Flexibility is how far a muscle can lengthen; mobility is how well you can actively control a joint through its range. Mobility work - controlled joint circles, deep squat holds, hip and thoracic spine drills - helps you move better in lifts and daily life. A few minutes in your warm-up or on rest days is enough for most people."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I improve my balance?", "Exercises for better balance?", "Why is balance training important?"],
     "Balance improves with practice: single-leg stands, single-leg deadlifts, step-ups, lunges and slow heel-to-toe walking all help. Start near a wall or chair for support, then make it harder by closing your eyes or standing on a softer surface. Balance training is especially valuable as you get older to help prevent falls."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I improve my posture?", "Exercises to fix bad posture?", "How can I stop slouching?"],
     "Posture improves by strengthening your upper back and core and moving more often. Include rows, face pulls or band pull-aparts, planks and glute bridges, stretch your chest and hip flexors, and break up long sitting with short movement breaks every 30-60 minutes. If posture comes with persistent pain, see a physiotherapist."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Are machines or free weights better?", "Free weights vs machines?", "Should I use machines at the gym?"],
     "Both work. Machines are easier to learn and good for isolating muscles or training safely alone; free weights train balance and stabilizing muscles and carry over well to daily movement. Many programs combine them - free-weight compound lifts plus machines for extra volume. Choose what you can do safely and consistently."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a full-body workout?", "Full body vs split routine?", "Should I do full body or a split?"],
     "A full-body workout trains all major muscle groups in each session, usually 2-3 times a week - ideal for beginners and busy people. A split trains different muscle groups on different days (for example upper/lower or push/pull/legs) and suits people training 4 or more days a week. Both build muscle if total weekly sets are similar."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a push pull legs split?", "How does PPL work?", "Explain the push/pull/legs routine"],
     "Push/pull/legs splits training into push days (chest, shoulders, triceps), pull days (back, biceps, rear shoulders) and leg days (quads, hamstrings, glutes, calves). Run it 3 days a week, or 6 days by repeating it twice. It suits intermediate lifters who want more volume per muscle group."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is an upper lower split?", "How does an upper/lower routine work?"],
     "An upper/lower split alternates upper-body days (chest, back, shoulders, arms) and lower-body days (quads, hamstrings, glutes, calves, core), usually 4 days a week. Each muscle group is trained twice per week, which works well for building strength and muscle once you've outgrown a beginner full-body plan."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a superset?", "How do supersets work?", "Are supersets effective?"],
     "A superset is two exercises done back to back with little rest - often opposing muscles like a push and a pull (for example push-ups then rows). Supersets save time and keep your heart rate up. Avoid pairing two very demanding lifts that use the same muscles, because form tends to slip."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is circuit training?", "How do I do a circuit workout?"],
     "Circuit training moves through several exercises in a row - for example squats, push-ups, rows, lunges and a plank - with short rests, then repeats the circuit 2-4 times. It combines strength and cardio and is time-efficient. Use moderate weights and keep good form even when you're breathing hard."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What does reps in reserve mean?", "What is RIR?", "What is RPE in training?"],
     "Reps in reserve (RIR) is how many more good reps you could have done at the end of a set - 2 RIR means you stopped two reps before failure. RPE is a 1-10 effort scale, where RPE 8 roughly equals 2 reps in reserve. Most working sets should land around 1-3 RIR (RPE 7-9)."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is tempo in lifting?", "Does lifting speed matter?", "Should I lift slowly?"],
     "Tempo is the speed of each phase of a rep. Lowering the weight under control (about 2-3 seconds) and lifting it with purpose builds strength and muscle while keeping you safe. Very slow reps aren't necessary - just avoid dropping the weight or using momentum to swing it up."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is time under tension?", "Does time under tension build muscle?"],
     "Time under tension is how long a muscle works during a set. It matters somewhat, but the bigger drivers of muscle growth are working close to failure and progressive overload. Controlled lowering and full range of motion are the practical ways to use it."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it okay to do the same workout every time?", "Should I change my workout routine often?", "How often should I change exercises?"],
     "Sticking with the same main exercises for 6-12 weeks is good - it lets you practise them and measure progress. Change the weights, reps or sets over time rather than the exercises themselves. Swap exercises when you plateau, get bored, or something causes discomfort."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What muscles should I train together?", "Which muscle groups go well together?"],
     "Common pairings are chest with triceps and shoulders (push), back with biceps (pull), and legs with core. Full-body sessions or upper/lower days also work well. What matters most is training each muscle group about twice a week with enough recovery between hard sessions."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I build stamina?", "How can I improve my endurance?", "How do I stop getting out of breath so fast?"],
     "Build stamina with regular cardio: mostly easy, conversational-pace sessions to build a base, plus one shorter, harder session each week (intervals or tempo work). Increase total time by about 10% per week, and be patient - aerobic fitness improves noticeably within 4-8 weeks of consistent training."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is VO2 max?", "How do I improve VO2 max?"],
     "VO2 max is the maximum amount of oxygen your body can use during intense exercise - a key marker of cardiorespiratory fitness. You can improve it with consistent aerobic training plus intervals, such as 3-5 minute hard efforts with equal recovery, once or twice a week, on top of an easy-cardio base."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a good resting heart rate?", "Does my resting heart rate show fitness?"],
     "For most adults a resting heart rate between about 60 and 100 beats per minute is considered normal, and it often drops lower as aerobic fitness improves. Track it first thing in the morning over time rather than comparing single readings. If yours is unusually high or low with symptoms like dizziness, check with a doctor."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What heart rate zone should I train in?", "What are heart rate zones?", "How do I use heart rate for cardio?"],
     "Heart rate zones are ranges of intensity. Easy, conversational cardio (roughly zone 2) builds your aerobic base and should be most of your cardio. Harder zones are used for intervals and tempo work. If you don't have a monitor, use the talk test: easy pace means you can speak in full sentences."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Can I get fit at home?", "Is working out at home as good as the gym?", "Do I need a gym to get fit?"],
     "Yes, you can get very fit at home. Bodyweight exercises (squats, lunges, push-ups, rows, planks) plus a pair of dumbbells or resistance bands cover all major muscle groups. Make them harder over time with more reps, slower tempo, harder variations or heavier weights, and add walking, running or cycling for cardio."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I start running?", "Running tips for beginners?", "How do I begin jogging?"],
     "Start with run-walk intervals - for example 1 minute easy jogging and 2 minutes walking, repeated for 20-30 minutes, 3 times a week. Gradually increase the running portions over several weeks. Keep the pace easy enough to talk, wear comfortable running shoes, and increase weekly time by no more than about 10%."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is yoga good exercise?", "Does yoga count as strength training?", "What are the benefits of yoga?"],
     "Yoga improves flexibility, mobility, balance and body awareness, and many people find it helps with stress. Some styles build decent strength and endurance, but for full benefits combine it with resistance training that you progressively load, plus some cardio."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is Pilates good for core strength?", "What does Pilates do?"],
     "Pilates focuses on controlled movement, core strength, posture and flexibility. It's a good complement to other training and can help with body awareness and stability. For bigger strength gains and bone health, combine it with progressive strength training."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Should I stretch before or after a workout?", "When is the best time to stretch?"],
     "Before a workout, do a dynamic warm-up (leg swings, arm circles, walking lunges) rather than long static holds. Static stretching - holding stretches for 15-30 seconds - fits best after training or as a separate session when your muscles are warm."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Can exercise improve my mood?", "Does working out help with stress?", "Is exercise good for mental health?"],
     "Yes - regular physical activity is linked to better mood, lower stress and anxiety, and better sleep. Even a 10-20 minute walk can lift your mood. If you're struggling with persistent low mood or anxiety, exercise can help alongside, but not instead of, support from a healthcare professional."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I fit exercise into a busy schedule?", "I don't have time to work out", "Quick workouts for busy people?"],
     "Short sessions count. Try 20-30 minute full-body circuits 3 times a week, take the stairs, walk during calls, and do 10-minute 'exercise snacks' like a set of squats and push-ups between tasks. Schedule workouts in your calendar and keep a minimal home routine for busy days."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What should I wear to the gym?", "Do I need special shoes for working out?"],
     "Wear comfortable, breathable clothing that lets you move freely. For lifting, flat, stable shoes work well; for running, use running shoes that feel comfortable. Make sure laces are tied and nothing loose can catch on equipment."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it safe to lift heavy weights?", "Will lifting heavy hurt my joints?"],
     "Lifting heavy is safe for most healthy people when you build up gradually, use good technique, warm up, and use spotters or safety bars for heavy presses and squats. Avoid sudden big jumps in weight and stop if you feel sharp or joint pain. If you have an existing injury or condition, get advice from a professional first."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Will lifting weights make women bulky?", "Do women get bulky from weights?"],
     "No - building large amounts of muscle takes years of focused training and eating, and women typically build muscle more slowly. Strength training helps women get stronger, firmer and healthier, with better bone density and metabolism. Train with progressive overload without worrying about getting 'bulky'."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Can I spot reduce belly fat with ab exercises?", "Do crunches burn belly fat?", "How do I lose fat from one area?"],
     "Spot reduction doesn't work - you can't choose where your body loses fat. Ab exercises strengthen the core muscles, but fat loss comes from an overall calorie deficit, regular exercise, enough protein and sleep. Over time fat decreases all over, including the belly."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is the afterburn effect?", "What is EPOC?"],
     "The afterburn effect (EPOC) is the extra energy your body uses after exercise to recover. It's real but fairly small - usually a modest addition to the calories burned during the workout itself. Focus on the total amount of exercise you do consistently rather than on afterburn."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is NEAT?", "Does non-exercise activity matter?"],
     "NEAT (non-exercise activity thermogenesis) is the energy you burn through daily movement outside workouts - walking, standing, chores, fidgeting. It can make a big difference to your total daily energy use, so walking more and sitting less is a useful habit for weight management."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is sitting all day bad even if I exercise?", "How do I deal with sitting at a desk all day?"],
     "Long periods of sitting are linked to health risks even in people who exercise. Break up sitting every 30-60 minutes with a short walk or a few stretches, use a standing break during calls, and aim to accumulate more daily steps on top of your workouts."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I avoid gym injuries?", "Tips to stay injury free when training?"],
     "Warm up first, learn good technique with light weights, increase load gradually, and leave a couple of reps in reserve. Use spotters or safety bars for heavy lifts, sleep and recover well, and don't train through sharp or joint pain. If an injury doesn't settle, see a physiotherapist or doctor."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a good beginner fitness goal?", "How should I set fitness goals?", "What goals should I set at the gym?"],
     "Set specific, measurable goals you control - for example 'train 3 times a week for 8 weeks', 'walk 8,000 steps a day' or 'do 10 full push-ups'. Pair a longer-term goal with weekly habit goals, track them, and adjust every month or two."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it bad to work out late at night?", "Will exercising at night ruin my sleep?"],
     "Evening exercise is fine for most people. Moderate workouts rarely harm sleep, but very intense sessions within an hour or so of bedtime can make it harder for some people to fall asleep. If that happens to you, finish hard training a bit earlier or keep late sessions lighter."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Should I exercise on an empty stomach?", "Is fasted cardio better?", "Can I work out before breakfast?"],
     "You can. Fasted training doesn't burn significantly more body fat over time than fed training - total weekly activity and diet matter more. Some people feel fine training fasted for light sessions; for long or intense workouts, a small snack 30-60 minutes before usually helps performance. Drink water either way."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a calorie deficit?", "How does a calorie deficit work?"],
     "A calorie deficit means eating fewer calories than your body uses, so it draws on stored energy (mostly fat) to make up the difference. A modest deficit of roughly 300-500 kcal a day leads to steady, sustainable loss of around 0.5-1% of body weight per week while keeping energy and muscle."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is metabolism?", "Can I speed up my metabolism?"],
     "Metabolism is all the energy your body uses - for basic functions, digesting food and moving. You can raise it modestly by building muscle, moving more during the day (walking, standing), eating enough protein and sleeping well. Big claims about 'metabolism-boosting' foods or pills aren't supported by good evidence."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How important is sleep for fitness?", "Does sleep affect muscle growth?"],
     "Very important. Most adults need 7-9 hours of sleep. Good sleep supports muscle repair, hormone balance, appetite control, focus and workout performance, and too little sleep makes training feel harder and recovery slower. Keep a consistent schedule and a dark, cool, quiet bedroom."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is overtraining?", "How do I know if I'm overtraining?", "Signs I'm training too much?"],
     "Overtraining (or more commonly under-recovery) happens when training stress outpaces recovery. Signs include lasting fatigue, falling performance, poor sleep, irritability, frequent illness, and soreness that doesn't go away. Take a few easier days or a deload week, prioritise sleep and food, and see a doctor if symptoms continue."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is the best workout for overall health?", "What's the best type of exercise?"],
     "The best workout is a mix you'll do consistently: about 150-300 minutes of moderate cardio per week, strength training for all major muscle groups at least twice a week, some balance and mobility work, and plenty of daily movement. Pick activities you enjoy so you stick with them."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Can I exercise during my period?", "Is it OK to train on my period?"],
     "Yes - exercise is generally safe and may even ease cramps and improve mood. Adjust intensity to how you feel: some days you may feel strong, on others lighter training or walking feels better. If you have very painful or heavy periods, speak with a doctor."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Do I need supplements to get fit?", "Are supplements necessary for working out?"],
     "No - most people can meet their needs with a balanced diet. Supplements are optional; the best-studied for training are protein powder (a convenient way to reach your protein target) and creatine monohydrate. Check any supplement with a doctor or pharmacist if you have a medical condition or take medication."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is creatine?", "Is creatine safe?", "Should I take creatine?"],
     "Creatine monohydrate is one of the most researched supplements and can modestly improve strength and power in short, intense efforts. A typical dose is 3-5 g per day. It's considered safe for healthy adults, but people with kidney problems or medical conditions should talk to a doctor first. It's optional - training and diet matter far more."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a pre-workout supplement?", "Do I need pre-workout?"],
     "Pre-workout products usually contain caffeine plus other ingredients. They're not necessary; a coffee or simply a good meal and sleep can do the same job. If you use one, check the caffeine content, avoid it late in the day, and skip it if you're sensitive to stimulants or have heart or blood pressure issues."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I breathe while lifting?", "What's the right breathing technique for weights?"],
     "A simple rule: breathe in before or during the lowering phase and breathe out as you push or lift. For heavy compound lifts, take a breath and brace your core before the rep, then exhale near the top. Avoid holding your breath for long periods, especially if you have high blood pressure."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is core strength?", "Why is a strong core important?"],
     "Your core includes the abdominal muscles, obliques, lower back, glutes and deep stabilizers around your spine. A strong core helps you transfer force in lifts, protects your back, and improves posture and balance. Train it with planks, dead bugs, bird dogs, carries and anti-rotation work, not just crunches."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it OK to lift weights every day?", "Can I lift weights daily?"],
     "You can lift most days if you rotate muscle groups - for example a push/pull/legs or upper/lower split - so each group gets about 48 hours before being trained hard again. Lifting the same muscles hard every day usually limits recovery and progress."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I get stronger?", "What's the best way to build strength?"],
     "Train compound lifts like squats, deadlifts, presses, rows and pull-ups 2-4 times a week, mostly in the 3-8 rep range with longer rests (2-3 minutes), and add weight gradually while keeping good form. Eat enough protein and calories, and sleep well. Strength comes from steady, consistent practice."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What does it mean to be fit?", "How do I know if I'm fit?"],
     "Fitness has several parts: cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength and endurance, flexibility and mobility, balance, and body composition. Being fit means you can handle daily life and your chosen activities with energy to spare. Simple checks include how easily you climb stairs, your resting heart rate, and how many push-ups or squats you can do with good form."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it normal to feel tired after working out?", "Why am I exhausted after the gym?"],
     "Feeling pleasantly tired after a hard workout is normal. Extreme exhaustion, or fatigue that lasts into the next days, often means the session was too hard, or you're short on sleep, food or fluids. Ease the intensity, eat a balanced meal with protein and carbs after training, and rest well. See a doctor if tiredness is persistent or unusual."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I come back to exercise after a long break?", "Returning to the gym after months off?"],
     "Start at roughly half your previous volume and weights, focus on technique, and build back over 2-4 weeks. You'll regain fitness faster than it took to build originally ('muscle memory'). Expect some soreness at first, and if the break was due to illness or injury, get clearance from a healthcare professional."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What is a training split?", "How should I organise my weekly workouts?"],
     "A training split is how you divide your workouts across the week - full-body (2-3 days), upper/lower (4 days) or push/pull/legs (3-6 days). Choose based on how many days you can train consistently, and make sure every major muscle group is trained about twice a week."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What equipment do I need to start working out?", "What's the minimum gym equipment for home?"],
     "You can start with no equipment at all. If you want to add some, a pair of adjustable dumbbells, a few resistance bands and a mat cover most needs; a pull-up bar and a sturdy bench are useful next steps. Good shoes and a water bottle round it out."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it bad to only do cardio?", "Can I just do cardio and no weights?"],
     "Cardio alone is great for heart health and endurance, but you'll miss the benefits of strength training - more muscle, stronger bones and joints, better metabolism and easier daily tasks. Add at least two short strength sessions a week alongside your cardio."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it bad to only lift weights and skip cardio?", "Do I need cardio if I lift?"],
     "Lifting builds strength and muscle, but regular cardio adds heart and lung fitness, endurance and recovery capacity. Aim for some cardio across the week - even brisk walking counts - toward the 150-300 minutes of moderate activity guideline."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["How do I avoid getting bored with exercise?", "Working out is boring, what can I do?"],
     "Mix things up: try new activities like cycling, swimming, classes, hiking or a sport, change your music or route, work toward a specific goal or event, and train with a friend. Keep the core of your program stable but rotate some exercises every few months."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Does stretching prevent injury?", "Will stretching stop me getting hurt?"],
     "Static stretching alone isn't strongly proven to prevent injuries. A dynamic warm-up, gradual increases in training load, good technique, strength training and enough recovery do more to reduce injury risk. Stretching is still useful for flexibility and feeling less stiff."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["What are the signs of dehydration during exercise?", "How do I know if I'm dehydrated while training?"],
     "Watch for thirst, dark yellow urine, headache, dizziness, fatigue, muscle cramps and a drop in performance. Drink regularly through the day and during longer sessions, especially in heat. If you feel confused, very dizzy or faint, stop, cool down and seek medical help."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it okay to exercise in hot weather?", "How do I train safely in the heat?"],
     "Yes, with care: train in cooler parts of the day, wear light clothing, drink water before, during and after, and lower your intensity at first while you adapt over 1-2 weeks. Stop and cool down immediately if you feel dizzy, nauseous, confused or stop sweating - heat illness needs urgent attention."),
    (G, "general_fitness", ["Is it okay to exercise in cold weather?", "Tips for working out in the cold?"],
     "Yes - dress in layers you can remove, protect your hands, ears and head, warm up a bit longer indoors first, and stay hydrated (you still lose fluid in cold air). Be careful on icy surfaces. People with heart or breathing conditions should check with a doctor about cold-weather exercise."),
]
